## Example

In the selected charge-one positive-chirality/neutral negative-chirality model, choose a smooth compactly supported cutoff "'$'"\\chi"'$ that equals $1$ on a neighborhood of the support of a nonnegative smooth compactly supported gauge parameter $'"\\alpha"'$. Let $'"\\lambda>0"'$ have mass dimension two, set $A='"\\lambda\\chi(x)(x"'^0dx''^1+x''^2dx''^3)$, and normalize $'"\\alpha"'$ so that $'"\\lambda"'^2'"\\int_M\\alpha\\,d"'^4x=1$. Then the consistent heavy-sector variation is nonzero and equals $-1/(12'"\\pi"'^2)$, and the Wess–Zumino term has the same variation for every $M_H$ satisfying the stated low-energy regime $E'"\\ll M_H"'$.

## Example references

[[pthm-aqft-heavy-chiral-threshold-wess-zumino-match|Conditional local threshold-matching theorem]]

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The theorem'"'s charge, transformation, and anomaly convention, together with the stated compact-support cutoff and normalized test parameter.

[F1] [[pthm-aqft-heavy-chiral-threshold-wess-zumino-match]]: the heavy-sector consistent variation and Wess–Zumino variation are both "'$-'"\\frac{1}{24\\pi"'^2}'"\\int_M\\alpha F\\wedge F"'$.

[A1] The support of $'"\\alpha"'$ lies where $'"\\chi=1"'$, $'"\\alpha\\geq0"'$, and $'"\\lambda"'^2'"\\int_M\\alpha\\,d"'^4x=1$.

## Verification

**Given:** [F1], [A1], and the orientation $dx''^0'"\\wedge dx"'^1'"\\wedge dx"'^2'"\\wedge dx"'^3$.

1.1 On a neighborhood of $'"\\operatorname{supp}\\alpha"'$, $A='"\\lambda(x"'^0dx''^1+x''^2dx''^3)$, so $F=dA='"\\lambda(dx"'^0'"\\wedge dx"'^1+dx''^2'"\\wedge dx"'^3)$. The cutoff makes $A$ globally smooth and compactly supported. [given, A1, construct, algebra]

1.2 The two summands of $F$ are degree-two forms, so they commute under the wedge product; each self-wedge is zero. Hence $F'"\\wedge F=2\\lambda"'^2dx''^0'"\\wedge dx"'^1'"\\wedge dx"'^2'"\\wedge dx"'^3$ on $'"\\operatorname{supp}\\alpha"'$. [step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 Integrating against $'"\\alpha"'$ and using [A1] gives $'"\\int_M\\alpha F\\wedge F=2\\lambda"'^2'"\\int_M\\alpha\\,d"'^4x=2$. Therefore the theorem'"'s anomaly and Wess–Zumino variation both equal "'$-2/(24'"\\pi"'^2)=-1/(12'"\\pi"'^2)$. [F1, A1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
