 Let $\psi_+$ and $\psi_-$ be complex positive- and negative-chirality Weyl fields in the trivial complex line bundle, of $U(1)_X$ charges $1$ and $0$, respectively. Let $\Phi=v e^{i\theta}$ be a nonvanishing order-parameter field with fixed modulus $v>0$, real phase $\theta\in C_c^\infty(M,\mathbb R)$, and charge $1$. A dimensionless nonzero Yukawa parameter $y$ gives the mass term $-y\Phi\,\bar\psi_+\psi_-+\mathrm{h.c.}$ and heavy mass $M_H=|y|v>0$. The fermion fields have mass dimension $3/2$, $v$ and $M_H$ have mass dimension one, $A_\mu$ has mass dimension one, $F_{\mu\nu}$ has mass dimension two, and $\theta$ is dimensionless.
 
-For $\alpha\in C_c^\infty(M,\mathbb R)$, use $A\mapsto A+d\alpha$, $\psi_+\mapsto e^{i\alpha}\psi_+$, $\psi_-\mapsto\psi_-$, and $\Phi\mapsto e^{i\alpha}\Phi$, hence $\theta\mapsto\theta+\alpha$. The covariant derivative on charge $q$ is $D_q=d-iqA$. Fix the Pauli–Villars consistent-current representative of the local anomaly and add no non-gauge-invariant local counterterm to the heavy determinant; gauge-invariant local counterterms may be added. The selected Wess–Zumino functional is the local four-dimensional functional proportional to $\int_M\theta F\wedge F$ whose coefficient is determined by the theorem on this page. This definition adopts formal perturbative determinant data only; it asserts no exact functional integral, continuum limit, or expansion remainder bound.
+For $\alpha\in C_c^\infty(M,\mathbb R)$, use $A\mapsto A+d\alpha$, $\psi_+\mapsto e^{i\alpha}\psi_+$, $\psi_-\mapsto\psi_-$, and $\Phi\mapsto e^{i\alpha}\Phi$, hence $\theta\mapsto\theta+\alpha$. The covariant derivative on charge $q$ is $D_q=d-iqA$. Let $\Gamma_H[A,\theta]$ denote the formal one-loop effective-action contribution of this fermion pair under the selected Pauli–Villars prescription: in the chiral-current triangle, subtract the loop amplitude of an auxiliary regulator fermion of opposite statistics and positive regulator mass before taking that mass to infinity. Define the consistent anomaly as the infinitesimal variation of this effective action, rather than as the divergence of a covariant current. Fix its representative by the resulting consistent-current convention and add no non-gauge-invariant local counterterm to $\Gamma_H$; gauge-invariant local counterterms may be added. For an undetermined real coefficient $c$, the candidate Wess–Zumino functional is $\Gamma_c[A,\theta]=c\int_M\theta F\wedge F$; the theorem on this page fixes $c$. This definition adopts formal perturbative determinant data only; it asserts no exact functional integral, continuum limit, or expansion remainder bound.
 
