+Use the heavy chiral $U(1)_X$ model with $M_H=|y|v>0$, background curvature $F=dA$, transformations $A\mapsto A+d\alpha$ and $\theta\mapsto\theta+\alpha$, and the fixed consistent-anomaly counterterm convention defined on this page. Adopt the source-derived Pauli–Villars consistent-anomaly formula for a positive-chirality Weyl field in a representation $R$:
+
+$$\delta_\alpha\Gamma_R=-\frac{1}{24\pi^2}\int_M\operatorname{tr}_R\!\left(\alpha_R\,d\left(A_R\wedge dA_R-\frac{i}{2}A_R\wedge A_R\wedge A_R\right)\right),$$
+
+where $A_R$ and $\alpha_R$ are the connection and infinitesimal parameter acting in $R$. For the one-dimensional charge-one representation, $A_R=A$, $\alpha_R=\alpha$, and $\operatorname{tr}_R(1)=1$; the cubic term vanishes because $A$ is an Abelian scalar one-form. Thus the heavy-sector variation is $\mathcal A_H(\alpha,A)=-\frac{1}{24\pi^2}\int_M\alpha F\wedge F$. If $\Gamma_L$ is any specified remaining-light-sector functional with variation $\mathcal A_L$, and the low-energy theory retains $\theta$ at external momenta $E\ll M_H$ and is required to match this perturbative background anomaly, then the local term
 
 $$\Gamma_{\mathrm{WZ}}[A,\theta]=-\frac{1}{24\pi^2}\int_M\theta F\wedge F$$
 

