## Example

Take the index-one Euclidean $S^4$ background, one massless left-handed Weyl doublet of a nondynamical $SU(2)$ global symmetry, and the order-two central transformation $z=-I$ specified in the global-anomaly test. Assume the source-supported chiral index is $1$ and pair the boundary determinant/Pfaffian line with the five-dimensional bulk whose test-loop phase is $(-1)^{\operatorname{ind}_2D_5}$. The boundary line has character $\chi(1)=1$, $\chi(z)=-1$; the mapping-torus mod-two index is $1$, so the bulk character is $\chi^{-1}=\chi$ and the combined character is trivial. The local index-density anomaly polynomial is zero. For the finite normalization check take a single-vertex complex $K$ and the ordinary field groupoid $\mathcal F(K)=BC_2$, with one object and automorphism group $C_2$. The combined unit weight is arrow-invariant and its normalized groupoid sum is $1/2$. Without the bulk, the odd boundary character is not a scalar invariant weight and no such finite gauging sum is defined. For two identical copies the boundary character itself is trivial and the same normalized groupoid sum applies. This example tests only the stated loop and does not establish that even copies have no other anomalies.

## Example references

[Wang, Wen, and Witten, §2.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1810.00844v4)

[Freed, Lecture 11, §§11.1–11.2](https://abel.math.harvard.edu/~dafr/CBMS133.pdf)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The conditional index-one SU(2) determinant/Pfaffian model, the order-two line character, the mod-two-index bulk response, and the finite single-vertex groupoid specified in the Example.

[F1] [[pthm-aqft-global-anomaly-test]] computes boundary and bulk phases for the same model and loop, conditional on the explicit odd-index input.

[F2] [[def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy]] defines the line holonomy as a unit-modulus scalar for the chosen regularization.

[F3] [[def-aqft-anomaly-inflow-relative-theory]] requires inverse boundary/bulk holonomies for cancellation.

[F4] [[def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration]] defines the normalized sum over isomorphism classes with reciprocal automorphism weights for an invariant function on a finite groupoid.

[A1] $C_2=\{1,z\}$ with $z^2=1$; the boundary line is the one-dimensional character $\chi(z)=-1$, while the inverse bulk character is $\chi^{-1}$.

[L1] Wang, Wen, and Witten, §2.2, PDF pp. 6–9, give the stated source model and mapping-torus sign calculation. The index input remains explicit rather than being claimed as a local index-theorem proof.

## Verification

1.1 Since $z^2=1$ and $\chi(z)^2=(-1)^2=1$, the assignment $\chi(1)=1$, $\chi(z)=-1$ is a character of $C_2$. Its inverse equals itself, so the tensor product of the boundary and bulk lines has the trivial character. [given, A1, algebra]

1.2 The single-vertex groupoid $BC_2$ has exactly one isomorphism class and its automorphism group has order $2$. The combined unit weight is constant on its arrows by step 1.1, so [F4] gives $Z=1/2$. The boundary-only character is not arrow-invariant, so it is not an admissible scalar weight for that sum. [given, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 For two identical boundary copies the character is $\chi^2=1$, and the mod-two bulk phase is also $(-1)^{2\cdot1}=1$. Thus the same finite groupoid sum has unit invariant weight and value $1/2$; this verifies cancellation for the stated test without asserting absence of any other anomaly. [given, F1, F3, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎

