items/pthm-aqft-heavy-chiral-threshold-wess-zumino-match.md:72:[F3] [[def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data]]: the selected group is $H=SU(3)\times U(1)_V$, and the one-loop local anomaly for the finite positive-chirality Weyl spectrum is an adopted descent input from the normalized degree-six polynomial. Its $U(1)_V$ BRST rules are $sB=d\eta$ and $s\psi_i=-\rho_{i*}(i\eta)\psi_i$; for the selected charge-one singlet this is $s\psi_+=-i\eta\psi_+$. Together with the finite transformations in [F1], these rules fix the ghost replacement $\alpha\mapsto\eta$ in the same real-parameter convention. The descent law remains an adopted source-backed input, not a locally derived theorem about every regulator.
items/pthm-aqft-heavy-chiral-threshold-wess-zumino-match.md:74:[A1] [Bilal's consistent positive-chirality anomaly formula](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.0634): use $\delta_\epsilon\Gamma_R=-\frac1{24\pi^2}\int\operatorname{tr}_R(\epsilon\,d(\mathscr A\wedge d\mathscr A-\frac i2\mathscr A\wedge\mathscr A\wedge\mathscr A))$ in the displayed Hermitian convention, without a non-invariant local counterterm. The model's explicit map is $\mathscr A=-B$, $\epsilon=-\alpha$.
items/pthm-aqft-heavy-chiral-threshold-wess-zumino-match.md:86:1.2 In Bilal's Hermitian convention [A1], $\mathscr A=-B$ and $\epsilon=-\alpha$. The ghost replacement $\alpha\mapsto\eta$ in [F1], [F3] gives the same infinitesimal transformation; in Bilal's anti-Hermitian variables this is $-i\mathscr A=iB$ and $-i\epsilon=i\alpha$, matching the product-group charge-one connection. For the positive-chirality $SU(3)$ singlet of $U(1)_V$ charge one in [F3], $\operatorname{tr}_R(1)=1$ and $d(\mathscr A\wedge d\mathscr A)=d(B\wedge G)=G\wedge G$; the Abelian cubic term vanishes. Therefore $\delta_\alpha\Gamma_H=+\frac{1}{24\pi^2}\int_M\alpha G\wedge G=\mathcal A_H(\alpha,B)$. The neutral opposite-chirality partner contributes zero $U(1)_V$ cubic charge. [given, F1, F3, A1, algebra]
